  "query_preview": "/Tuesday through Saturday/...\nIssued 148 PM MST SAT NOV 21 2026\nThere is solid agreement amongst ensemble members that the active \nweather pattern will continue through Tuesday, before a warming and \ndrying trend takes hold for the Wednesday into Friday. This trend \nisn't fully backed up by deterministic runs, with the GFS and ECMWF \nalong with ECMWF-AIFS showing more of a moist flat ridge pattern \nsetting up Wednesday into the weekend. These differences are \nintroducing a significant amount of uncertainty into the forecast \nfor the latter end of next week. Both scenarios do point to some \nwarming for the area, with snow levels creeping upward to 5000-6000 \nfeet by Friday. This would ensure that most valley locations would \nremain snow free, with accumulations for the higher mountain valleys \nand peaks. A wrinkle that could influence where valleys end up is \nhow strong of an inversion sets up under the ridging for the late \nweek. A stronger ridge scenario would help to trap cold air into \nvalleys, including the Long Valley near McCall, which could lower \nthe snow level to valley floor there, even though the upper level \npattern would support the higher snow levels mentioned earlier. This \ncould impact holiday travel in and through the mountains. Model \ntrends have been leaning toward the wetter solutions of a flat ridge \nscenario, with longer rain views showing a return to a series of \ntroughs by early the following week. Needless to say, stay tuned, \nespecially if you have holiday travel plans this week.",
